.Dd February 11, 2026
.Dt CPIO 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm cpio
.Nd copy file archives in and out
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Fl o
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl H Ar format
.Op Fl F Ar archive
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Nm
.Fl i
.Op Fl t
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl n
.Op Fl dmu
.Op Fl H Ar format
.Op Fl F Ar archive
.Op Fl -safe-extract
.Op Fl -no-absolute-paths
.Op Fl -no-overwrite
.Op Fl -no-preserve-owner
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Op Fl f
.Op Fl E Ar file
.Op Ar pattern ...
.Nm
.Fl p
.Op Fl v
.Op Fl dlmu
.Op Fl -no-preserve-owner
.Op Fl R Ar owner
.Ar directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility reads and writes cpio archives using copy-out
.Pq Fl o ,
copy-in
.Pq Fl i ,
and pass-through
.Pq Fl p
modes.
.Pp
Pathnames for
.Fl o
and
.Fl p
are read from standard input, one per line.
.Pp
With
.Ar pattern
operands, copy-in (and
.Fl t )
takes only the members whose names match one of them, as
.Xr fnmatch 3
matches with no flags: a
.Ql *
matches a
.Ql /
too.
.Pp
Pass-through copies regular files, directories, symbolic links and
fifos, and, when run by root, device files; the names of one file are
copied as links to one copy.
A socket is reported and left out.
.Pp
Copy-in and pass-through give each file the archived (or source)
permissions exactly, set-user-ID, set-group-ID and sticky bits
included; the umask does not apply.
A directory is made writable by its owner at first and given its own
mode, and with
.Fl m
its time, only once everything has been extracted or copied, so that a
read-only directory still takes its contents.
.Pp
Copy-out writes a file with more than one link in each format's way:
newc names it with every name but the last at size zero and puts the
data with the last, holding the names back until all of them (or the
end of the list) have come; odc and bin put the data with every name.
.Pp
Copy-in restores hard links: the names an archive gives one file (the
same device and inode number, with a link count above one) are
extracted as links to one file.
The data may come with the first name, with every name, or, as newc
archives have it, with the last; names still waiting for it at the end
of the archive become an empty file and links to it.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl o
Create archive.
.It Fl i
Extract archive.
.It Fl p
Pass-through copy to directory.
.It Fl F Ar archive
Read/write archive file instead of stdin/stdout.
.It Fl H Ar format
Archive format: bin, odc, newc.
A file larger than the format can record is left out with a
diagnostic: 4\~GiB less one byte for newc and bin, 8\~GiB less one for
odc.
.It Fl t
List the archive's members, one name per line on standard output,
rather than extracting them.
The extraction safety checks do not apply to a listing.
.It Fl v
Verbose output: name each file as it is archived, copied or extracted,
on standard error.
With
.Fl t ,
list each member as
.Xr ls 1
.Fl l
does: mode, links, owner, group, size (or major and minor numbers),
modification time and name, and for a symbolic link its target.
.It Fl n
With
.Fl tv ,
show the owner and group as numbers.
.It Fl V
Write a dot to standard error for each file archived, extracted or
copied, and a newline at the end
.Pf ( Fl -dot ) .
.It Fl d
Create leading directories.
.It Fl m
Preserve modification time on extraction/copy.
.It Fl u
Replace files unconditionally.
Without it, a file already on disk that is no older than the one
replacing it is kept, with the diagnostic
.Dq not created: newer or same age version exists
(which does not change the exit status).
.It Fl R Ar owner
On copy-out, record this owner in the archive; on copy-in and
pass-through, give it to every file made.
.Ar owner
is
.Ar user : Ns Ar group ,
.Ar user . Ns Ar group ,
.Ar user
alone (the group is left as it is),
.Ar user :
(the user's login group) or
.Pf : Ar group ;
each a name or a number.
.It Fl c
The same as
.Fl H Cm newc ,
as in GNU cpio.
.It Fl I Ar archive
With
.Fl i ,
read
.Ar archive
rather than standard input.
.It Fl O Ar archive
With
.Fl o ,
write
.Ar archive
rather than standard output.
.It Fl 0
Read the name list of
.Fl o
and
.Fl p
as names each ended by a NUL byte rather than a newline, as
.Ql find -print0
writes them
.Pf ( Fl -null ) .
.It Fl L
With
.Fl o
and
.Fl p ,
take what each symbolic link points to rather than the link itself
.Pf ( Fl -dereference ) .
.It Fl a
With
.Fl o
and
.Fl p ,
put back each file's access time after reading it
.Pf ( Fl -reset-access-time ) .
.It Fl B
Use blocks of 5120 bytes rather than 512.
.It Fl C Ar size
Use blocks of
.Ar size
bytes
.Pf ( Fl -io-size ) .
.It Fl -block-size Ns = Ns Ar n
Use blocks of
.Ar n
\(mu 512 bytes.
.It Fl -quiet
Do not report the number of blocks.
.It Fl D Ar directory
Change to
.Ar directory
first
.Pf ( Fl -directory ) .
.It Fl f
With
.Fl i ,
take the members that match none of the patterns instead.
.It Fl E Ar file
With
.Fl i ,
read patterns from
.Ar file ,
one per line, as well as from the operands.
.It Fl l
With
.Fl p ,
link each file to its source rather than copying it, where they are
on one file system; copy it otherwise.
.It Fl -safe-extract
Refuse members whose names have a
.Sq ..
component, and hold back the archive's own symbolic links whose
targets are absolute or have a
.Sq ..
component until the whole archive has been read, so that no member is
written through one.
Until then an empty file holds each such link's name.
Symbolic links already on disk are followed as usual.
This is the default.
.It Fl -no-absolute-paths
Refuse members with absolute names.
This is the default.
.It Fl -absolute-paths
Allow members with absolute names.
.It Fl -insecure
Turn off both of the above: extract absolute names, names with
.Sq ..
components, and the archive's symbolic links as they come.
.It Fl -no-overwrite
Do not replace existing files on extraction.
.It Fl -no-preserve-owner
When run by root, leave the files made on copy-in and pass-through
owned by root rather than giving them the archived (or source) owner,
which is otherwise the default.
.It Fl -numeric-owner
Restore the archived owner by number, which is what a cpio archive
records; this is the default when run by root.
.El
.Pp
The options also have GNU cpio's long names:
.Fl -create ,
.Fl -extract ,
.Fl -pass-through ,
.Fl -list ,
.Fl -verbose ,
.Fl -file ,
.Fl -format ,
.Fl -make-directories ,
.Fl -preserve-modification-time ,
.Fl -unconditional ,
.Fl -link ,
.Fl -nonmatching ,
.Fl -pattern-file ,
.Fl -owner
and
.Fl -numeric-uid-gid ;
.Fl -no-absolute-filenames
and
.Fl -absolute-filenames
are
.Fl -no-absolute-paths
and
.Fl -absolute-paths .
Exactly one of
.Fl o ,
.Fl i
and
.Fl p
is given
.Pf ( Fl t
alone means
.Fl it ) ,
and the options come before the operands;
.Ql --
ends them.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It 0
Success.
.It 1
Non-fatal errors occurred (partial success).
.It 2
Fatal error.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Each diagnostic is written to standard error and begins
.Ql cpio: ,
then the name of the file or member concerned.
.Pp
Copy-out writes the archive in whole blocks, the last padded with zeros.
At the end of a run that did not fail,
.Nm
reports on standard error how many blocks it wrote, read or (for
.Fl p )
copied, as
.Dq Ar n Li blocks ,
unless
.Fl -quiet
is given.
.Pp
On copy-in, bytes that are not a header where one is due are skipped to
the next header, with a warning of how many; a malformed header is
reported and passed over.
An archive that ends before its trailer is a fatal error:
.Dq premature end of archive
if no header was found at all,
.Dq premature end of file
otherwise.
.Pp
On copy-out, a file that yields fewer bytes than its size when it was
examined is padded with zeros to that size, with a warning, so that
the archive stays readable.
A failure to write the archive is fatal.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pax 1
.Sh STANDARDS
Behavior follows POSIX cpio model with pragmatic safety extensions.
